{
  "abstract": "For e, n >= 1 let S_e(n) be the mean of the least residues of the e-th powers modulo n, s_e(n) = #{k < n : n | k^e} and defect_e(n) = (n - s_e(n))/2 - S_e(n). The development proves, for every e and every n >= 1, the divisor form defect_e(n) = sum_{d | n} G_e(d), where G_e(d) is a sawtooth sum of the Moebius-inverted counts of e-th roots modulo d. For odd e it gives G_e = 0, so that S_e(n) = (n - s_e(n))/2. For every e >= 1 and n >= 1 it gives the closed form G_e(n) = - sum_tau [psi_tau odd] B_1(psi_tau) prod_{p | n} W_p(tau), a sum over choices of local Dirichlet characters chi_p modulo prime powers p^c dividing n, with explicit local weights built from psi_tau(p). Only the odd primitive characters with psi^e = 1 contribute. The proof is elementary: - root counts modulo prime powers (dilation, vanishing, small levels); - character orthogonality for the unit counts; - changeLevel for the primitive part; - the inflation, dilation and parity properties of the sawtooth functional. For e = 2 the result is the formula of the MeanResidue release, which is Proposition A.2 (a = 1) of Skoruppa and Zagier. For prime moduli the evaluation is classical. No statement for e >= 3 and composite n was found in the literature. Nothing is quoted.",
